Based on my analysis of the radiograph — the periapical regions of the heavily restored maxillary anterior teeth and the mandibular molar apices show areas of altered trabecular bone structure with subtle mineral loss (no clearly well‑defined radiolucent cavity), consistent with PAI 3.

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.252, 0.475, 0.320, 0.565], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.640, 0.465, 0.720, 0.555], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.450, 0.360, 0.512, 0.432], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.524, 0.358, 0.586, 0.430], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Note on confidence: this panoramic is heavily processed/upscaled and the periapical bone is busy with overlapping sinus, nasal, and trabecular detail. I found no lesion with a sharply defined radiolucent cavity (PAI 4/5), so all reported findings are the subtle PAI 3 category, at the mandibular molar apices bilaterally and at the apices of the restored maxillary anterior teeth.
